caymanslm: never BUG() in the SELinux policy-evaluation paths

Upstream SELinux assumes the policy is loaded once and never mutated, and on that
assumption sprinkles BUG()/BUG_ON() through the hot evaluation paths as
"cannot happen" assertions. KernelSU breaks that assumption: it performs live
surgery on the policydb (adds types, grows type_attr_map_array, inserts avtab and
xperm nodes, deep-copies class datums and their constraint expressions).

Every one of those assertions runs under read_lock(&policy_rwlock) with preemption
disabled, so ANY inconsistency becomes an unrecoverable atomic-context panic that an
unprivileged app can trigger simply by causing the right permission check. That is
not an acceptable failure mode for a kernel that mutates its own policy.

Convert them all to fail closed -- deny the permission / skip the refinement and
pr_warn_once() -- instead of killing the machine:

  - context_struct_compute_av()          type_attr_map slots (every perm check)
  - services_compute_xperms_decision()   type_attr_map slots + xperms specified
  - security_bounded_transition()        type_datum lookup (every exec transition)
  - type_attribute_bounds_av()           source/target type_datum
  - constraint_expr_eval()               6 BUG()s + stack depth asserts
  - context_struct_to_string()           NULL symbol names
  - permission-value range assert

All guards fail CLOSED: an early return leaves avd->allowed = 0, and an unmet
constraint masks permissions off, so nothing is ever wrongly allowed.

NOTE: on this device none of these guards has ever fired -- a validator confirmed
the policydb is structurally intact (nprim=3356, zero NULL slots in any of the three
type arrays). They are kept as hardening of a genuine defect class, NOT as a fix for
the com.eltavine.duckdetector panic, which remains unexplained. See
tests/baselines/duckdetector-kernel-panic-UPSTREAM-REPORT.md.

